    verification receiptAtMeansTheSameInBothReceiptsRetroGate : Test { :>> id = "63c2f363-64d8-4562-ab5f-3a10acc232fe"; :>> title = "Sprint 667 retro gate (autonomous, D0049)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-09-11"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus5"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::analyze; :>> procedureText = "AVOIDABLE-ISSUE SCAN: one new Issue this sprint, issue477 (FINDING 3); issue476 (recorded at the close of sprint 666 - the recorder's evidence began with the receipt marker the write path adds itself, and four lines carried it twice) is carried by dcEvidenceReceiptMarkerIsIdempotent. FINDING 1: the verifier's rule at > launch was written for the touched receipt and applied to the suite receipt by the skill that dispatches both; the skill compensated with a filesystem derivation rather than the receipt being fixed, for two sprints. A reader's workaround written into procedure is a defect deferred: the fix belonged in the writer the first time the skill had to explain the difference. FINDING 2: facts.py held the same START claim in a how string a page reader sees; doc-sync found it by grepping the receipt's filename, which is the test that should run for any receipt-shape change (every reader names the file). FINDING 3 (D0425): no source edit was made while the previous sprint's land compiled, because the land builds from the working tree and include_dir embeds .engine at compile time - an edit mid-run would have been tested as the previous commit's. That constraint is in no control, so it is issue477 with resolver dcLandTestsTheCommitItPushes: the touched receipt fingerprints the deliverable before and after cargo and land refuses a run whose tree moved or whose start differs from the committed tree - the D0425 primary should not have to hold its edits by discipline for the length of a land."; }
